#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148725 :tutorial/content "We can represent $3$-dimensional shapes in $2$-dimensions using **nets**.\nTo make a polyhedron net, we take its faces and lay them out as if they were lying flat and open.\n\nBelow, we have the net of a polyhedron, but which one?\n\n![](images/a9/a9484094d29f4f74d383d7f669d71be291f7cf9906ed59a377dea71c95f7be66.png)\n\nWe can get back the original polyhedron by folding the net along its edges to form a solid shape. The way we fold the net is shown below:\n\n![](images/67/6797f9f6e1203167e4ebaaec0179c6309ac2fe2f908ef8ec36a5866e2890b974.png)\n\nAs a result, we get a cube:\n\n![](images/df/dfaec291ab1abf049146d58975c22461df721c82739ab672f47f91a47a19a9eb.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d764b3fc-d40c-537b-b042-70913f5f0beb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2279 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
